@startuml fixed-point
' The fixed point of the Y.
'
' The Y of the name is the Y-combinator: person -> environment -> agent ->
' environment.  This picture gives that sentence a shape, and ties it to the
' one thing of the kernel that makes it a loop: the delegation.  A child of a
' delegation runs in a session of its own, with a journal of its own, with the
' tools of its preset and at a depth of its own.
'
' The limits are on the picture on purpose: the depth of the tree is bounded,
' the time of a child is bounded and the budget of a child is bounded.  A
' recursion that is not bounded is not a fixed point, it is a fork bomb.
'
' The fixed point is the last step of the picture: the agent changes the code
' and the check that hold it, and the check passes afterwards.
'
' Every line of the drawing is wrapped at twenty-eight characters or so: the
' picture must fit the column of the page at the font size the palette names,
' and a wider picture is scaled down until its words are smaller than the
' words of the page (the check
' `check-the-pictures-are-readable-in-the-column.el' measures it).

|Human|
start
:give the task\nto the environment;

|Environment|
:holds the files,\nthe buffers\nand the checks\nof the project;

|Agent|
:read the environment;
:call the tools of the kernel\n(read, edit, bash,\ngit_status, todo_list);
:change the environment\n(the live state of Emacs\nis read and written here)\nlib/yeah-emacs.el:62\nlib/yeah-emacs.el:120;

|Environment|
:the change is\nthe new environment;

|Agent|
if (does the task\nneed a child?) then (yes)
  :delegate names an agent preset\n(the preset is a file:\na name, a model,\nthe tools, the depth)\nlib/yeah-agent.el:131\nlib/yeah-delegate.el:219;
  :the child runs in a session of\nits own, with a journal of its\nown, its own tools and its own\ndepth (the tree depth, the time\nand the budget of a child are\nbounded)\nlib/yeah-subagent.el:156\nlib/yeah-subagent.el:319;
  :the child changes\nthe environment too;
else (no)
  :the agent keeps\nthe tools of its own;
endif

|Human|
:see what\nthe environment holds;

|Agent|
:run the check of the environment;
if (does the check fail?) then (yes)
  :read the failure\nand change the\nenvironment again;
else (no)
  :the environment holds the\nrepair: the agent fixed its\nown code and its own check;
endif
stop
@enduml
